ase concerns the removal of a Federal Reserve governor due to alleged deceit or gross negligence in financial transactions, specifically regarding mortgage applications and interest rates.\n  - id: 24-1238, name: Montgomery v. Caribe Transport II, LLC, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a state law claim against a transportation broker can be preempted by federal law, which would prevent the claim from being pursued.\n  - id: 24A884, name: Trump v. CASA, Inc., description: The case concerns the constitutionality of Executive Order 14,160, which protects American citizenship, and the use of universal injunctions by district courts to block government actions.\n  - id: 25-332, name: Trump, President of United States v. Slaughter, description: The case concerns the President\'s power to remove and supervise executive branch officials, with Petitioners arguing that a Supreme Court decision known as Humphrey\'s Executor should be overturned because it allows federal agencies to wield significant power without being accountable to the President.\n  - id: 20-322, name: Garland, Att\'y Gen. v. Gonzalez, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a lower court can issue a class-wide injunction to stop certain immigration provisions, and if so, under what circumstances.\n  - id: 22-381, name: Yegiazaryan v. Smagin, description: The case Yegiazaryan v. Smagin concerns whether a U.S.-based plaintiff can sue under the civil RICO law for treble damages after suffering an injury abroad, specifically in Russia. The dispute centers on the interpretation of a Supreme Court precedent and whether it allows foreign plaintiffs to bring claims in U.S. courts.\n  - id: 19-635, name: Trump v. Vance, description: The case concerns whether a county district attorney can issue criminal process against a sitting President of the United States, with the Supreme Court to decide if temporary presidential immunity is constitutionally required. The issue at hand involves a subpoena for the President\'s tax returns, with implications for presidential power and local accountability.\n  - id: 19-508, name: AMG Capital Management, LLC v. FTC, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether the Federal Trade Commission can seek a permanent injunction under Section 13(b) of the FTC Act, or if it must use a different provision to obtain monetary relief for past consumer harm.\n  - id: 19-351, name: Federal Republic of Germany v. Philipp, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether a U.S. court can hear claims against foreign sovereigns for human rights and law of war violations, or if the expropriation exception only applies to property claims.\n  - id: 23-939, name: Trump v. United States, description: The case concerns whether a sitting president can be prosecuted for their official actions, with the petitioner arguing that such prosecution would undermine the presidency and the separation of powers.\n  - id: 20-366, name: Trump v. New York, description: The case concerns the President\'s authority to determine how to count individuals in the country illegally for purposes of apportioning congressional seats, with the President arguing he has discretion to decide who is a resident and should be counted.\n  - id: 18-1447, name: Republic of Hungary v. Simon, description: The case concerns whether a US court can hear claims against Hungary for property confiscations during World War II, despite the US having previously settled its own claims with Hungary and the potential for international friction.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\